## Abstract

Tasks described as taking “five minutes” frequently require substantially more than five minutes to complete. Despite this, the estimate continues to be used in both personal and professional settings.

This paper investigates the phenomenon by examining several common examples of five-minute tasks, including sending an email, putting something away, making a cup of tea, and finding a file. In each case, the initial estimate was compared with the actual amount of time required.

The results indicate that the original five-minute estimate was generally optimistic. Additional time was attributed to preparation, distraction, unexpected complications, and the discovery of unrelated tasks.

The authors conclude that five minutes should not be considered a reliable unit of task duration, although it remains a socially acceptable estimate.

## Introduction

People frequently describe tasks as taking approximately five minutes.

The phrase is usually used with confidence.

“I'll just do this quickly.”

“This should only take five minutes.”

“I'll be done in five minutes.”

These statements are often made before the task has begun and therefore before sufficient evidence exists to determine how long the task will actually require.

The present study examines why five-minute tasks so frequently exceed their estimated duration.

## Methodology

A number of ordinary tasks were observed and assigned an estimated completion time before beginning.

Tasks included:

- sending a short email;
- putting an object away;
- finding a document;
- making a cup of tea;
- cleaning one small area;
- changing a setting on a device;
- and locating an item that was believed to be nearby.

Participants were instructed to begin each task immediately and record when the task was considered complete.

No attempt was made to control for distractions.

This was considered representative of normal conditions.

## Results

The majority of tasks exceeded their initial five-minute estimate.

Several recurring patterns were identified.

### Preparation

The task itself was often preceded by preparation.

For example, sending an email required opening the relevant application, locating the recipient, remembering the purpose of the email, and occasionally discovering that the application required an update.

The preparation was not included in the original estimate.

### Distraction

Participants frequently encountered other information while attempting to complete the original task.

A participant attempting to find a document might notice another document.

The second document might raise a question.

The question might require a search.

The search might reveal an unrelated subject of interest.

At this point, the original five-minute task had ceased to be the only task being performed.

### Unexpected Complications

Tasks described as simple frequently contained additional steps.

A cupboard could be opened only to reveal that the intended object had been placed behind another object.

A file could be found only to discover that it was the wrong file.

A setting could be changed only after locating the correct settings menu.

The additional steps were not generally included in the original estimate.

### Task Expansion

The most significant finding was that tasks appeared capable of expanding during execution.

A participant intending to clean one small area might notice another area.

The second area might appear easier to clean at the same time.

Additional areas could then become visible.

The task was therefore no longer “clean the small area.”

It had become “clean several things.”

The original five-minute estimate remained unchanged.

## Discussion

The findings suggest that the five-minute estimate is frequently made under idealised conditions.

The estimated task is usually the task as imagined.

The completed task is the task as it actually exists.

These are not necessarily the same task.

For example, “send an email” may refer to typing and sending the email.

In practice, it may involve remembering why the email needs to be sent, finding the necessary information, opening the computer, discovering that the computer is performing an update, checking another notification, responding to that notification, returning to the email, and finally remembering why the email was being sent.

The original estimate does not appear to account for this.

This suggests that the five-minute task may not actually be a task with a duration of five minutes.

It may instead be a task with an optimistic description.

## Conclusion

The five-minute task remains a persistent feature of everyday life despite repeatedly exceeding its predicted duration.

The authors recommend that future estimates account for preparation, distraction, unexpected complications, and the possibility that the task may become several other tasks.

However, the continued use of five-minute estimates is not expected to decline.

The authors therefore recommend further research into whether a task described as taking “ten minutes” is simply a five-minute task that has already learned from experience.

Further investigation may be warranted.
